    "body": "AI assisted 🤖\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\n\r\nLSP servers sometimes send `null` for fields that the spec marks as\r\nnon-nullable (e.g. `typeDefinitionProvider: null` from psalm, `tags: null` in\r\ndiagnostics). Neovim's JSON decoder converts JSON `null` to `vim.NIL` — a\r\ntruthy userdata value — which bypasses `or {}` guards and causes crashes in\r\nindividual handlers like `tags_lsp_to_vim`. Fixing this spot-by-spot is fragile\r\nand doesn't scale.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nUse the LSP meta-model to auto-generate a dispatch table of validator functions\r\n(`_validate.lua`), keyed by method name, and call the appropriate validator in\r\n`rpc.lua` before params reach any handler. This catches unexpected `null`\r\nvalues at a single, central point rather than in each handler individually.\r\n\r\nThe validators currently operate in lenient mode: a `vim.NIL` value in a\r\nnon-nullable field is repaired in-place (set to `nil`) and a warning is logged\r\nto `:log lsp`, deduplicated per client and field so a chatty server produces at\r\nmost one log entry per unique offending field.\r\n\r\nOpen questions / known gaps:\r\n\r\n- **Recursion depth**: validators follow nested struct references up to a\r\nconfigurable depth (currently 2); the right default needs more thought.\r\n- **Strict vs. lenient mode**: strict mode (raise an error instead of\r\nrepairing) exists but was too aggressive in practice — needs a design decision.\r\n- **Logging**: the current warning mechanism may need refinement to avoid being\r\nnoisy or hard to discover.\r\n- **Coverage**: some param types are not yet validated due to added complexity.\r\n- **Performance**: validating every incoming message has not been benchmarked\r\nyet.\r\n- **Tests**: coverage needs to be extended.",
    "closed_at": "2026-06-29T13:08:24Z",
    "comment_data": [],
