            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> be more specific about which is `nvim`'s response where the incorrect behavior is experienced\r\n\r\nThe error described in #40390 is:\r\n\r\n```\r\nvim.schedule callback: ...ped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:74: bad argument #1 to 'ipairs' (table expected, got userdata)\r\nstack traceback:\r\n        [C]: in function 'ipairs'\r\n        ...ped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:74: in function 'tags_lsp_to_vim'\r\n        ...ped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:125: in function 'fn'\r\n        vim/_core/shared:286: in function 'diagnostic_lsp_to_vim'\r\n        ...ped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:253: in function 'handle_diagnostics'\r\n        ...ped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:266: in function 'handler'\r\n        ...wrapped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/client.lua:1319: in function '_notification'\r\n        ...wrapped-0.12.3/share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/client.lua:470: in function 'fn'\r\n        [string \"vim/_core/editor\"]:273: in function <[string \"vim/_core/editor\"]:272>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere is a minimal reproduction.\r\n\r\n**`~/.config/nvim_40465/init.lua`**\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.lsp.log.set_level(\"debug\")\r\nvim.lsp.config(\"psalm\", {\r\n\tcmd = { \"/home/kim/.local/share/nvim/mason/bin/psalm-language-server\" },\r\n\tfiletypes = { \"php\" },\r\n\troot_markers = { \"psalm.xml\" },\r\n})\r\nvim.lsp.enable(\"psalm\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**`/tmp/40465/psalm.xml`**\r\n```xml\r\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\r\n<psalm errorLevel=\"1\">\r\n  <projectFiles>\r\n    <directory name=\".\"></directory>\r\n  </projectFiles>\r\n</psalm>\r\n```\r\n\r\n**`/tmp/40465/test.php`**\r\n```php\r\n<?php\r\nfunction add(int $a, int $b): int { return $a + $b; }\r\nadd(\"not an int\", 2);\r\n```\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n/tmp/40465 via 🐘 v8.5.5\r\n❯ NVIM_APPNAME=nvim_40465 nvim test.php\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis immediately triggers the error above.\r\n\r\n**Why it crashes**\r\n\r\nThe LSP log (`~/.local/state/nvim_40465/lsp.log`) shows what psalm sends for `textDocument/publishDiagnostics`:\r\n\r\n```\r\n\"rpc.receive\"  { jsonrpc = \"2.0\", method = \"textDocument/publishDiagnostics\", params = {\r\n  diagnostics = { {\r\n    ...\r\n    tags = vim.NIL\r\n  } },\r\n  uri = \"file:///tmp/40465/test.php\",\r\n  version = 0\r\n} }\r\n```\r\n\r\nPsalm sends `\"tags\": null` in JSON. Neovim's JSON decoder converts `null` → `vim.NIL`, a non-`nil` sentinel. Because `vim.NIL` is truthy in Lua, the guard in `diagnostic.lua` fails:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- runtime/lua/vim/lsp/diagnostic.lua:86\r\nfor _, tag in ipairs(diagnostic.tags or {}) do\r\n--                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n--  vim.NIL is truthy, so this evaluates to vim.NIL instead of {}\r\n--  ipairs(vim.NIL) → \"bad argument #1 to 'ipairs' (table expected, got userdata)\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe LSP spec marks `Diagnostic.tags` as `DiagnosticTag[] | undefined` — sending `null` is a server bug, but it happens in practice (psalm does it; reported upstream at vimeo/psalm#11895). Neovim currently has no defense against it.\r\n\r\nThe log also shows other fields carrying `vim.NIL` in the same diagnostic (`codeDescription`, `relatedInformation`) and in the server capabilities (`codeLensProvider`, `renameProvider`, etc.). Those don't crash today, but they represent the same class of problem: optional fields sent as `null` instead of being omitted, leaving `vim.NIL` values scattered through structures that handlers may not expect.",
